      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's dive into the technical explanation.\n\nThe pitot tube is essentially a static pressure probe that measures the air pressure at the front of the aircraft. When air flows past the probe, it creates a pressure difference between the air flowing into the tube and the static air pressure. This pressure difference is then transmitted to the aircraft's flight control system, which uses it to calculate the airspeed.\n\nNow, if the pitot tube becomes blocked, it can cause a significant problem. The blocked tube prevents the pressure sensor from detecting the air pressure difference, which in turn prevents the aircraft from accurately calculating its airspeed. This can lead to a range of issues, including:\n\n* Incorrect airspeed readings, which can cause the aircraft to be flown at an incorrect speed\n* Loss of control authority, as the aircraft's flight control system relies on accurate airspeed data to make decisions\n* Increased risk of stall, as the aircraft may be flown at an incorrect speed, leading to a stall warning or even a stall\n\nIn extreme cases, a blocked pitot tube can lead to a loss of control, which can be catastrophic. Pilots need to be aware of the potential risks and take steps to",
