      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A blocked pitot tube is often caused by ice or frost, which can form on the tube due to cold temperatures. This is because the pitot tube is typically exposed to the cold air at the front of the aircraft, and if the air is cold enough, it can cause the water vapor in the air to freeze onto the tube.\n\nIn fact, ice buildup on the pitot tube is one of the most common causes of a blocked pitot tube. The ice can form in a few different ways, including:\n\n* Freezing of water vapor in the air\n* Condensation of water vapor on the tube\n* Freezing of moisture that has accumulated on the tube\n\nBugs or debris can also cause a blockage, but ice is by far the most common culprit. In some cases, a blocked pitot tube can be caused by other factors, such as:\n\n* Dirt or debris accumulating on the tube\n* Corrosion or damage to the tube\n* Incorrect installation or maintenance of the pitot tube\n\nBut ice is usually the main culprit, and pilots need to be aware of the risks of ice buildup on the pitot tube, especially in cold weather conditions.",
